ENGLISH 83

&
\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% RULE RSentNegVar
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ I1:mu1,
              I3:mu3
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ I1:mu1,
              negrel/NEGVAR{NEGVARrec1},
              I3:mu3
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ALL (mu1, I11:rel1/..) 
         I11: QUOTE_SRelPrec(rel1, negrel) AND
              NOT rel1 IN [negrel, meltnegrel, posrel] 
       I3: ALL (mu3, I31:rel3/..) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu3, [predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[I4:mu4]])
         I31: QUOTE_SRelPrec(negrel, rel3) AND
              NOT rel3 IN [negrel, meltnegrel, posrel] 
         I4:  EXIST (mu4, [meltnegrel/..])
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TSentNegVar
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
x1 probably did NEGVAR hear x2, \\
(It was impossible) for x1 NEGVAR to see x2, \\
NEGVAR to go there (would be foolish), \\
x1 did NEGVAR ever solve x2 (They didn't ever solve it)\\
$^{*}$NEGVAR ever to go there (would be foolish) (this sentence is derived by 
the the following rule, RSentMeltNegVar)\\
$^{*}$But x1 do POSVAR NEGVAR shoot horses (no pos + neg)\\
$^{*}$NEGVAR x1 did NEGVAR lie (Nobody didn't lie; no meltneg + neg)\\
$^{*}$x1 did NEGVAR NEGVAR ever lie (he didn't never lie; no neg + meltneg)\\
$^{*}$x1 did NEGVAR [say NEGVAR anything] (no pos + VPneg)
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_PosNegVar
\item[* date of creation:] 1-JUN-1988 13:46:07  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] 
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 07/12/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Removed explicit predrel/VERBP from model, because tempadvrels follow the VERBP 
but may melt. (In fact, this only applies to next rule (meltnegrel), so the 
current rule could have been left unchanged, but there is no harm in the 
change).
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to mark the place where a NEG that is not going to melt with 
anything in the S may be inserted during the 
substitution rules, and to provide a VAR to guide the order of substitution
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) In its current 
formulation, this rule will be applicable only once.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
