\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RSentMeltNegVar
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ I1:mu1,
              I3:mu3
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ I1:mu1,
              meltnegrel/NEGVAR{NEGVARrec1},
              I3:mu3
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       m1: ALL
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu1, [I11:rel1/..])
         I11: rel1 IN [posrel, negrel]
       I3: QUOTE_firstisapossnietNP(mu3) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu3, [I31:rel3/..]) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu3, [predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[I4:mu4]])
         I31: rel3 IN [posrel, negrel, meltnegrel]
         I4 : EXIST (mu4, [meltnegrel/..])
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RSentMeltNegVar
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
NEGVAR Anything he could remember nowadays, \\
NEGVAR Anybody had seen her, \\
NEGVAR Anybody NEGVAR ever lies (Nobody never lies),\\
(It is unforgivable) for a man NEGVAR ever to bring his wife flowers, \\
??(It was impossible) for NEGVAR anyone to see me (perhaps this 
should be excluded only by the ForToInf Moodrule), \\
$^{*}$NEGVAR Anybody possibly came (pre-positioning of the sentadv is required 
here: possibly, nobody came)\\
$^{*}$x1 did NEGVAR NEGVAR ever come (x1 didn't never come)\\
$^{*}$x1 did POSVAR NEGVAR ever come\\
$^{*}$NEGVAR anybody did POSVAR come
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_PosNegVar
\item[* date of creation:] 1-JUN-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] 
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 07/12/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Removed explicit predrel/VERBP from model, because tempadvrels follow the VERBP 
but may melt. Also removed `sentadvrel' from set in matchcondition I31, because 
English does not use a sentadvrel yet at all.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to mark the place where a NEG that is going to melt with an 
element from S may be inserted during the 
substitution rules, and to provide a VAR to guide the order of substitution
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) Since the 
matchconditions specify that no meltnegrel may follow the meltnegrel inserted 
here, there is only one way to get two (or more) meltnegrels in S 
(generatively speaking: introduce the leftmost first).
